course: everyday
lesson: 383
title: Street gangs and loyalty
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Kieran's group told him that to «prove he was loyal» he had to throw a stone at a Station Road window. He did it, the glass cracked, and a toddler inside screamed. The group cheered and gave him a «gang name» — Razor — and for a few days Kieran felt ten feet tall. Then he got told Sam was «not loyal» because Sam wouldn't come to the big fight, and he found himself saying it too, even though Sam had been his mate since nursery.
explain: The other street was no better. Older Station Road boys tried to recruit Jake because he was «a good fighter», calling him «little bro» and buying him chips. Lily's brother started hanging around with the same older group, coming home late and snapping at everyone. Meanwhile Maddie was told «you're Mill Lane, so you have to fight» when a Station Road girl insulted the street — as if a rude word about a road was something she had to bleed for.
explain: Then came the scuffle. A Station Road boy shoved Maddie so hard she fell on the kerb and split her lip. Nobody from the group walked her home; they were too busy planning the «final battle» at the rec. Maddie sat on her doorstep holding a tissue to her mouth and thought: «Loyal to what, exactly?»
explain: Jake was the first to say it out loud. He told Kieran: «I'm out. A group that makes you smash windows and calls your mates traitors isn't loyal to you — it just uses you.» Real loyalty means sticking by people when they need you and being honest with them, not doing harm because a group demands it.
fact: A gang that asks you to «prove» yourself by doing something harmful or illegal is using you, not looking after you.
fact: Throwing stones at windows can injure people inside with flying glass, and it is a crime called criminal damage.
fact: Older teenagers sometimes «groom» younger children with gifts, praise and nicknames so the younger ones will do risky things for them.
fact: Real loyalty means caring for people and being honest with them, not obeying a group no matter what.
fact: Refusing to join a fight is not betrayal; a true friend does not ask you to get hurt or hurt others.
fact: Planned «final battles» rarely end anything; they usually lead to injuries, revenge and more fights.
fact: A split lip or a fall on a kerb can need a doctor, and head injuries can be serious.
fact: Leaving a group can feel scary, but trusted adults — parents, teachers, youth workers — can help.
fact: If you are worried a brother, sister or friend is being drawn into a gang, telling a trusted adult is the right thing, not snitching.
fact: A gang name can make someone feel important, but it does not make them safer or more respected by people who matter.
fact: If you know a big planned fight is coming, telling an adult or the police early can stop people getting hurt.
exercise: Kieran's group made him throw a stone at a window to «prove loyalty». What were they really doing? | Using him to do something harmful | Testing if he was brave in a fair way | Teaching him how to throw really well | Looking after him like a real family
exercise: What did the stone through the window cause? | A cracked window and a terrified toddler | Only a small noise, like a knock | Nothing, the window was empty | A fun game for the family inside the house
exercise: Why can stones through windows be very dangerous? | Flying glass can cut the people inside | Windows always bounce stones straight back | It only hurts if the window is open at the time | Stones never break modern double-glazed windows
exercise: How did getting the gang name «Razor» make Kieran feel? | Important for a while | Scared and sorry right away | Bored of the gang and its games | Angry with the gang for using him
exercise: Kieran called Sam «not loyal» for not joining the big fight. What is the truth? | Sam was being a good friend by not adding to the harm | Sam was a traitor to the whole of Mill Lane | Sam should have gone with them to prove himself | Sam was only staying away because he was scared of Kieran
exercise: Why did the older Station Road boys buy Jake chips and call him «little bro»? | To win him over so he would fight for them | Because they really cared about how his tea was | Because they were his real brothers | To say sorry to him for the street war
exercise: Lily's brother is coming home late and snapping. What should Lily do? | Tell a trusted adult she is worried | Keep it a secret so he is not in trouble | Join the group to keep an eye on him | Follow him at night to see where he goes
exercise: Telling an adult you are worried your brother is in a gang is… | The right thing, because it can keep him safe | Snitching, so you should never, ever do it | Only right if he has already been arrested | Something only parents are allowed to do
exercise: Maddie was told «you're Mill Lane, so you have to fight». What is true? | Nobody has to fight because of their street | Living on Mill Lane means you must defend it | She only has to fight girls | She only has to fight if someone insults her
exercise: After Maddie split her lip, how did the group treat her? | Nobody walked her home; they planned the next fight | They took her straight to the doctor in Kieran's mum's car | They all said sorry to her | They cancelled the battle and went home
exercise: What did Maddie's split lip show her about the group? | It did not really care about her | It would always protect her, whatever happened | It was the safest group in the whole town | It would pay for her dentist bill
exercise: What usually happens after a planned «final battle»? | Injuries, revenge and more fighting | Both streets are friends for ever | Nobody ever fights again | The winners get a prize from the council
exercise: Jake told Kieran he was leaving. What did he mean by «it just uses you»? | The group cared about what you'd do for it, not about you | The group lent him things when he needed them | The group was really a kind of sports team | The group wanted him to be their captain one day
exercise: Which is an example of real loyalty? | Walking a hurt friend home and getting help | Throwing a stone because your group says so | Calling a friend a traitor for not fighting | Joining the battle so your street wins
exercise: If you hear a «final battle» is planned at the rec, what is the wise thing? | Tell a trusted adult early so it can be stopped | Go along to watch so you know who won in the end | Bring friends to help your side | Keep quiet so no one calls you a grass
exercise: Why might leaving a gang feel scary? | You may fear being teased or threatened by the group | Gangs always let people go easily | Gangs never notice who leaves | Leaving a gang is against the law for children under twelve
exercise: Who can help a child who wants to leave a group like this? | Parents, teachers or youth workers | Only the gang leader | Nobody; you must do it alone | Only older kids from the other street
test: «if ur really one of us, chuck this at the window. go on, Razor». Best reply? | No. If that's what it takes, I'm out | OK but I'll aim for the frame, not the glass | OK, but only a little stone | OK if you do one too
test: Sam: «I'm not coming to the rec tonight». Kieran: «so ur a traitor then». What's actually true? | Sam's just not doing harm; that's not betrayal | Sam has let the whole street down, he's a traitor | Sam must be on Station Road's side | Sam's scared so he doesn't count
test: Older lad: «here's a fiver, little bro, just hold this bag for me». What's wise? | Say no and tell a trusted adult | Take it, a fiver's a fiver | Hold it but don't look inside the bag | Ask for a tenner instead
test: Mum: «Has your brother been out with them older lads again?» Lily knows he has. What's best? | Tell Mum the truth, because she can help him | Say no, to protect him from getting in trouble | Say she doesn't know anything | Say yes but beg Mum not to talk to him
test: «that Station Road girl called Mill Lane a dump, u gotta sort her out, Maddie». Wise answer? | It's just words, I'm not fighting over a road | Fine, after school by the shop, just you and me | Only if she says it again | I'll get Jake to do it
test: Maddie's lip won't stop bleeding after a fall on the kerb. What's sensible? | Press a clean cloth on it and get an adult; it may need a doctor | Lick it until it stops bleeding, then go back out | Ignore it and go back out | Put soil on the cut to dry it out quickly
test: «final battle Sat at the rec, ends it for good, trust». True? | No, it'll likely lead to more hurt and revenge | Yes, one big fight settles everything for good | Yes, if your side wins it | Only if the police don't come
test: Jake: «I'm out of the group». Kieran: «nobody leaves, mate». What should Jake do? | Stay out and tell a trusted adult if he's threatened | Go back in so there's no trouble for anyone | Fight Kieran in front of everyone to prove he's allowed to leave | Move house to get away
test: Kieran's mum hears kids calling him «Razor». She asks why. What should he say? | Tell the truth about the window so it can be put right | Say it's because he shaves | Say it's just a made-up game name, nothing more | Say he doesn't know
test: Lily's mate: «if u grass on ur brother he'll hate u». What's true? | Getting him help is caring, even if he's cross at first | He'll hate you for ever, so it's not worth it | Keeping quiet is always safer for him in the end | Only grass if he's arrested
test: Which friend is truly loyal? | The one who walks you home with a split lip | The one who wants you with them at every fight | The one who gives you a cool gang name | The one who calls others traitors for you
exam: Identify the principal purpose served when a group requires a younger member to commit damage in order to «prove loyalty». | The group secures the member's participation in harmful acts for its own ends | The group assesses the member's courage in a fair and impartial manner | The group protects the member from harm | The group instructs the member in lawful conduct
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of older youths bestowing gifts, praise and nicknames upon younger children? | It may constitute grooming intended to draw children into risky conduct | It constitutes disinterested kindness in all cases | It is required by custom between neighbouring streets in a town | It is without significance to the welfare of the child
exam: It is given that a pupil declines to attend a planned altercation between two streets. Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning loyalty. | The refusal is not betrayal, loyalty consisting in care and honesty towards persons | The refusal constitutes disloyalty to the pupil's street | Loyalty requires participation in every altercation involving one's street | The refusal is acceptable only if the pupil is injured
exam: State the action most consistent with the welfare of a child whose sibling appears to be associating with an older group engaged in fighting. | The communication of the concern to a trusted adult | The concealment of the matter in order to avoid conflict | The joining of the group in order to supervise the sibling | The pursuit of the sibling at night without informing anyone
